Good question. Let me think through this carefully.                                                                                \
                                                               \
  
\f1\b GSD should NOT always be as good or better than GS.
\f0\b0  They optimize different things:                                                \
                                                                                \
  - 
\f1\b GS
\f0\b0  (static): Plans all routes at DAG injection using HEFT timing estimates. It optimizes for 
\f1\b total system throughput
\f0\b0  across all  \
  transfers simultaneously, considering estimated concurrency windows.            \
  - 
\f1\b GSD
\f0\b0  (dynamic): Plans each route individually at transfer start time. It optimizes for 
\f1\b this flow's bottleneck bandwidth
\f0\b0  given the\
  current state.                                                                \
\
  The key problem is that 
\f1\b GSD
\f0\b0  
\f1\b is
\f0\b0  
\f1\b greedy
\f0\b0  
\f1\b per-transfer
\f0\b0  \'97 it picks the best path for each transfer in isolation, without considering how\
   that choice affects future transfers. This can lead to:\
\
  1. 
\f1\b Route
\f0\b0  
\f1\b conflicts
\f0\b0 : GSD might route multiple transfers through the same "currently best" path, creating congestion that a global\
  planner would have avoided by spreading traffic across paths.\
  2. 
\f1\b No
\f0\b0  
\f1\b look-ahead
\f0\b0 : GS sees the whole DAG and can coordinate routes. GSD only sees what's active right now, so it can't reserve\
  capacity for upcoming transfers.\
\
  However, there IS one case where GSD should strictly dominate: 
\f1\b when
\f0\b0  
\f1\b GS's
\f0\b0  
\f1\b timing
\f0\b0  
\f1\b estimates
\f0\b0  
\f1\b are
\f0\b0  
\f1\b wrong
\f0\b0  (which they often are, since\
  HEFT estimates don't account for interference). In those cases, GSD's real-time state should help.\
\
  The real question is whether GSD's scoring function is flawed. Let me check something.\
